<!-- ACT 1: Person in a moment + Impossible framing + Era Bridge -->
    <h2 id="summer">The Summer Parents Stopped Letting Their Children Outside</h2>

    <p>You wake up. Your child has a fever. It is July 1952. You do not know it yet, but your neighbor's son two houses down woke up with the same fever. Last week, the girl on the corner could not move her legs. The only thing the doctors can tell you is: "there is no cure."</p>

    <p>Polio hit the United States hardest in 1952. Imagine: fifty-seven thousand six hundred and twenty-eight cases. Twenty-one thousand two hundred and sixty-nine people paralyzed. Three thousand one hundred and forty-five dead. The numbers kept climbing through the summer. Swimming pools closed. Movie theaters shut. Parents stopped letting their children play outside. A fever was the only warning you got. If your child could not move their legs the next morning, you waited. You hoped. Most of the time, the paralysis stayed. Some of the time, the iron lung was the only thing that kept them breathing.</p>

    <p>You have never thought about polio. You have never checked your child's legs after a fever. That is the difference a vaccine makes.</p>

    <!-- ACT 2: The wrong turns -->
    <h2 id="iron-lung">The Iron Lung and the False Dawns</h2>

    <p>In 2026, you search symptoms on your phone. In 1952, you waited. That is what the 1950s offered. Parents formed neighborhood watch groups, not for crime, but for polio. Every case was a mystery. The virus spread through contaminated water, through feces, through the hands of an infected child who never showed symptoms. You could not see it coming. You could not stop it.</p>

    <p>However, scientists had been trying for decades. Franklin D. Roosevelt, the president of the United States, had been paralyzed by polio in 1921. He built a foundation to fight it. The March of Dimes collected money from every corner of the country. People mailed dimes to the White House. But the money did not create a cure. The best they had was the iron lung, a coffin-sized machine that breathed for you when your diaphragm muscles stopped working. It kept you alive. It did not fix you.</p>

    <p>However, there were false starts. In the 1930s, a vaccine made of infected monkey spinal cords caused paralysis in some children. It was abandoned. A killed-virus approach was tried but the virus was so dangerous that growing it in labs meant risking the lives of researchers. Most scientists believed a killed virus could never produce immunity. You needed a live, weakened virus, they argued. But weakening the virus without making it dangerous had never been done at scale.</p>

    <!-- ACT 3: The Breakthrough (The Fix) -->
    <h2 id="room-215">The Man in Room 215</h2>

    <p>Then came the breakthrough. Jonas Salk was not the obvious choice. He was a young virologist working out of a small lab in the basement of the University of Pittsburgh. Room 215. No big budget. No famous name. He had spent years developing an influenza vaccine using killed virus. While other scientists chased the live-attenuated approach, Salk quietly believed in a different path.</p>

    <p>He grew poliovirus in monkey kidney cells. Then he killed it with formaldehyde. The question was whether a dead virus could teach the immune system to fight a live one. Every leading virologist in the country, including Albert Sabin who later developed the oral vaccine, believed it could not. A killed virus would not trigger enough immunity. Salk disagreed. He tested the vaccine on himself. He tested it on his wife. He tested it on his three sons.</p>

    <p>In 1954, Salk's vaccine was ready for the largest field trial in medical history. One million eight hundred thousand children enrolled. They were called the "polio pioneers." Each child received either the vaccine or a placebo. The results were kept sealed. The country held its breath.</p>

    <p>On April 12, 1955, the results were announced. The vaccine was 80 to 90 percent effective. The news spread faster than polio ever had. Church bells rang. People wept in the streets. Jonas Salk became a national hero overnight. When a reporter asked him who owned the patent, he answered: "There is no patent. Could you patent the sun?"</p>

    <p>The vaccine was free. Salk never made a dollar from it.</p>

    <!-- ACT 3 Low point twist: The Cutter Incident -->
    <h2 id="disaster">The Disaster That Almost Destroyed Everything</h2>

    <p>However, two weeks after the announcement, disaster struck. Cutter Laboratories in California, one of the licensed manufacturers of the vaccine, released a batch with live poliovirus in it. The killed virus had not been fully killed. Two hundred thousand children received the faulty doses. Two hundred were paralyzed. Ten died.</p>

    <p>The vaccine program was suspended. Parents who had just celebrated the end of polio were suddenly terrified of the cure. The Surgeon General of the United States personally reviewed every batch. The investigation took months. The truth emerged: Cutter's production process was flawed, not Salk's science. The vaccine itself was safe. Production standards were rewritten. Every batch was tested three times before release.</p>

    <p>Here is the good news: the vaccine went back into distribution. By the end of 1955, six million children had been vaccinated. The US polio rate had already begun to fall.</p>

    <!-- ACT 4: The Result -->
    <h2 id="zero">From 57,000 to Zero</h2>

    <p>By 1961, Albert Sabin's oral polio vaccine was available. A sugar cube with a few drops of live weakened virus. Easier to administer. Cheaper to produce. It became the global standard. But polio was already retreating in the United States.</p>

    <p>In 1960, fewer than 3,000 cases. In 1970, fewer than 50. The last case of wild polio in the United States was recorded in 1979. A disease that had paralyzed 57,000 Americans in a single summer, gone in 27 years.</p>

    <p>But globally, polio was still a monster. In 1988, the World Health Organization, Rotary International, UNICEF, and the US Centers for Disease Control launched the Global Polio Eradication Initiative. Their target: every child on earth vaccinated. Their starting point: 350,000 cases in 125 countries.</p>

    <p>Rotary alone raised over 2 billion dollars. The Gates Foundation matched donations and poured in billions more. Volunteers walked through war zones in Afghanistan. They climbed mountains in Pakistan. They negotiated ceasefires to vaccinate children. They tracked every case of acute flaccid paralysis. They went house to house, village to village, room to room.</p>

    <p>Then the milestones started coming. By 2014, India was declared polio-free. A country of 1.4 billion people, where polio had been endemic for decades, had eliminated it. By 2020, Africa was certified wild polio-free. Three of the three wild poliovirus strains had been eliminated.</p>

    <p>Today, in 2026, wild polio exists in exactly two countries: Afghanistan and Pakistan. Twenty-three cases in Afghanistan this year. A handful in Pakistan. The virus is cornered.</p>

    <blockquote>"Polio eradication is one of the greatest public health achievements in history. We stand on the threshold of a world free of a disease that has paralyzed millions."<br>- World Health Organization (2025)</blockquote>

    <p>The honest part: the last mile is the hardest. Vaccine-derived polio outbreaks still occur in under-vaccinated communities. Political instability in Afghanistan makes vaccination campaigns dangerous. Health workers have been killed in the line of duty. A novel oral polio vaccine (nOPV2) was developed in 2023 to address vaccine-derived outbreaks. The endgame has been ongoing for years. But the trajectory is clear. One direction since 1988. Down.</p>

    <!-- ACT 5: The Lesson + You-Relevance -->
    <h2 id="never-seen">The Thing You Have Never Seen</h2>

    <p>Here is the part that matters. You have never seen polio. You have never seen a child in an iron lung. You have never worried about a fever in July because of what it might do to your child's legs. You have never seen an iron lung at all. Most people born after 1980 have never encountered a single case of polio in their lives.</p>

    <p>That is not normal. That is the result of a man who refused to patent the sun, a field trial involving 1.8 million children, a disaster that almost ended everything, and a global campaign that spent billions to chase a virus into the last two countries on earth where it still hides.</p>

    <p>Polio is not gone yet. But it is close. Closer than anyone thought possible in 1952, when the swimming pools were empty and the iron lungs were full. The lesson is not just about vaccines. It is about the kind of effort required to end something that has plagued humanity for millennia.</p>

    <p>Smallpox was eradicated with a forked needle and a smarter strategy. Polio is being eradicated with sugar cubes, ceasefires, and billions of dollars. The pattern is the same. Someone refuses to accept a problem as permanent. They find the tool that makes the math work. Then they work it, case by case, village by village, country by country, until the virus has nowhere left to hide.</p>

    <p>What are you telling yourself is too big to solve?</p>
